From 7f761340b76f8d7264f5e3c8ef5e20c03d1e75ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:27:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-backend: queue a CPU split as a host function in the GPU
 stream before it

In the gpu backend's layout every layer is a GPU split, then a CPU split
(the routed experts), then the next GPU split. The scheduler's thread
waited for the GPU after each GPU split, computed the CPU split, and only
then queued the next input copy and launched the next CUDA graph, while
the card waited (nsys: about 115 us from the end of the copy to the graph
start, a layer).

Now a CPU split whose inputs are all copies from the previous split's
backend runs as a host function in that backend's queue (new optional
backend call launch_host_func; cudaLaunchHostFunc in CUDA). The split
after it reads its outputs in copies queued behind it, so the scheduler's
thread queues every split of a verify step without waiting. A copy from
pinned host memory to a backend's own buffer no longer waits for that
backend first: it is queued behind the backend's earlier work anyway.
The thread waits asleep at the end and before any split that needs the
host work done.

Exact (same bits; test-backend-sched-host compares against the per-split
path). Measured on koishi: the gap before each graph drops from about
115 us to 6 us, but the steady verify step is not faster and the prompt
read is about 3% slower: the CPU split computes slower on CUDA's callback
thread (its own OpenMP team), and the callback adds latency.

